1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,679
A server is never idle. Something is always running.

2
00:00:04,229 --> 00:00:06,125
A process is a running program.

3
00:00:06,125 --> 00:00:07,349
When one misbehaves,

4
00:00:07,349 --> 00:00:08,573
you need to find it,

5
00:00:08,573 --> 00:00:09,062
read it,

6
00:00:09,062 --> 00:00:09,797
and stop it.

7
00:00:10,347 --> 00:00:14,613
Today you list processes, send signals, and check how busy the machine is.

8
00:00:16,663 --> 00:00:19,436
Start with the simplest list. Type: ps.

9
00:00:19,986 --> 00:00:25,682
Only your own shell, and P S itself. Every process has a number, the P I D, and a name.

10
00:00:26,632 --> 00:00:34,462
Now every process on the system, with the columns you choose. Type: ps -eo pid,user,comm.

11
00:00:35,012 --> 00:00:36,596
Three columns: the P I D,

12
00:00:36,596 --> 00:00:37,230
the owner,

13
00:00:37,230 --> 00:00:38,244
and the command.

14
00:00:38,244 --> 00:00:41,540
Process number one is the parent of everything else.

15
00:00:42,490 --> 00:00:49,722
Add C P U and memory. Type: ps -eo pid,pcpu,pmem,comm.

16
00:00:50,272 --> 00:00:56,373
Percent of one C P U, and percent of memory. Those two columns find a hungry process fast.

17
00:00:58,423 --> 00:01:01,085
Start a program in the background.

18
00:01:01,085 --> 00:01:02,494
Type: sleep 300 &.

19
00:01:02,494 --> 00:01:05,783
The ampersand returns your prompt at once.

20
00:01:06,333 --> 00:01:11,645
Bracket one is the job number. The next number is its P I D. Check with: jobs.

21
00:01:12,195 --> 00:01:13,441
Stop it politely.

22
00:01:13,441 --> 00:01:14,467
Type: kill %1.

23
00:01:14,467 --> 00:01:16,445
That sends the term signal,

24
00:01:16,445 --> 00:01:17,545
number fifteen.

25
00:01:17,545 --> 00:01:19,377
The program may clean up,

26
00:01:19,377 --> 00:01:20,110
then exit.

27
00:01:20,660 --> 00:01:22,964
Check again with: jobs. Terminated.

28
00:01:24,014 --> 00:01:32,227
Now a runaway program. Type: yes > /dev/null &. It burns a whole C P U doing nothing.

29
00:01:32,777 --> 00:01:33,827
Find it.

30
00:01:33,827 --> 00:01:35,666
Sort by C P U,

31
00:01:35,666 --> 00:01:38,685
and show the top three.

32
00:01:38,685 --> 00:01:45,513
Type: ps -eo pid,pcpu,comm --sort=-pcpu | head -n 4.

33
00:01:46,063 --> 00:01:47,491
Yes is at the top,

34
00:01:47,491 --> 00:01:49,474
near one hundred percent.

35
00:01:49,474 --> 00:01:51,457
Stop it with: kill -9 %1.

36
00:01:51,457 --> 00:01:53,836
Signal nine cannot be ignored.

37
00:01:53,836 --> 00:01:54,788
Use it last.

38
00:01:55,838 --> 00:01:57,640
Order of force: term first,

39
00:01:57,640 --> 00:01:59,242
kill only if term fails.

40
00:01:59,242 --> 00:02:02,580
Control C sends the int signal from your keyboard.

41
00:02:04,630 --> 00:02:07,275
How busy is the machine? Type: uptime.

42
00:02:07,825 --> 00:02:10,250
The three numbers are the load average,

43
00:02:10,250 --> 00:02:10,810
over one,

44
00:02:10,810 --> 00:02:12,364
five and fifteen minutes.

45
00:02:12,364 --> 00:02:15,100
Compare them with the number of C P U cores.

46
00:02:16,150 --> 00:02:18,369
Count the cores. Type: nproc.

47
00:02:18,919 --> 00:02:23,060
A load above the core count means work is waiting.

48
00:02:23,060 --> 00:02:23,972
Now memory.

49
00:02:23,972 --> 00:02:26,125
Type: free -h | column -t.

50
00:02:26,125 --> 00:02:28,113
Column tidies the table.

51
00:02:28,663 --> 00:02:30,452
Look at the available column,

52
00:02:30,452 --> 00:02:31,007
not free.

53
00:02:31,007 --> 00:02:33,042
Linux uses spare memory as cache,

54
00:02:33,042 --> 00:02:34,893
and gives it back when needed.

55
00:02:36,943 --> 00:02:41,615
Is a program running? Start one. Type: sleep 300 &.

56
00:02:42,165 --> 00:02:48,010
Now ask by name. Type: pgrep -a sleep. It prints the P I D and the command.

57
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:51,846
Stop everything with that name. Type: pkill sleep.

58
00:02:52,396 --> 00:02:55,361
Ask again. Type: pgrep sleep. No output.

59
00:02:55,911 --> 00:02:59,708
Read the exit status. Type: echo $?.

60
00:03:00,258 --> 00:03:01,989
One means nothing matched.

61
00:03:01,989 --> 00:03:03,120
Zero means found.

62
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:08,578
Scripts and monitoring tools use this exit status to test whether a service is up.

63
00:03:10,628 --> 00:03:11,951
Four myths to clear up.

64
00:03:12,501 --> 00:03:16,695
Myth one: kill dash nine is the normal way to stop a program.

65
00:03:16,695 --> 00:03:16,902
No.

66
00:03:16,902 --> 00:03:17,658
Term first,

67
00:03:17,658 --> 00:03:18,965
so it can clean up.

68
00:03:19,515 --> 00:03:25,211
Myth two: high memory use means a problem. Not always. Linux caches. Read available.

69
00:03:25,761 --> 00:03:29,212
Myth three: a process with a high load is the only suspect.

70
00:03:29,212 --> 00:03:31,435
Check the load against the core count.

71
00:03:31,985 --> 00:03:36,828
Myth four: kill only kills. It sends signals, and most signals ask politely.

72
00:03:37,378 --> 00:03:38,999
Term first. Kill last.

73
00:03:41,049 --> 00:03:43,374
Here is how you check your own understanding.

74
00:03:43,924 --> 00:03:47,039
List processes with P I D, user and command.

75
00:03:47,589 --> 00:03:49,936
Start a job in the background, and find it.

76
00:03:50,486 --> 00:03:53,814
Stop it with term, and say when kill dash nine is needed.

77
00:03:54,364 --> 00:03:56,881
Find the process using the most C P U.

78
00:03:57,431 --> 00:03:59,714
Read the load average against the core count.

79
00:04:00,264 --> 00:04:04,061
Check if a program runs with P grep, and read its exit status.

80
00:04:04,611 --> 00:04:06,232
To pass, you need all six.

81
00:04:08,282 --> 00:04:08,894
Your turn,

82
00:04:08,894 --> 00:04:10,364
in your virtual machine.

83
00:04:10,364 --> 00:04:11,834
These commands are safe,

84
00:04:11,834 --> 00:04:13,978
but only stop programs you started.

85
00:04:14,528 --> 00:04:19,883
One: list processes with P I D, user and command, and find process number one.

86
00:04:20,433 --> 00:04:24,785
Two: start a sleep in the background, and find it with jobs and with P grep.

87
00:04:25,335 --> 00:04:28,769
Three: stop it with a normal kill, and confirm it is gone.

88
00:04:29,319 --> 00:04:34,418
Four: start yes, sent to dev null, find it at the top by C P U, then stop it.

89
00:04:34,968 --> 00:04:39,960
Five: read uptime and nproc, and say in one sentence if your machine is busy.

90
00:04:40,510 --> 00:04:45,289
Six: check free dash h, and explain the difference between free and available.

91
00:04:45,839 --> 00:04:49,145
Mark yourself on the rubric. Pause the video, and do it now.

92
00:05:04,695 --> 00:05:05,570
Quick check.

93
00:05:06,120 --> 00:05:08,509
Which character starts a command in the background?

94
00:05:09,059 --> 00:05:11,022
Which signal does a plain kill send?

95
00:05:11,572 --> 00:05:13,406
Which memory column should you read?

96
00:05:13,956 --> 00:05:16,495
What exit status means a process was found?

97
00:05:17,045 --> 00:05:23,466
Objective covered: MC04-O05.

98
00:05:24,016 --> 00:05:26,086
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

99
00:05:36,636 --> 00:05:40,582
Next lesson: system D services, logs and scheduled jobs.

100
00:05:41,132 --> 00:05:44,460
